faqs:
  - question: "How do I add analytics to a mobile app with Appwrite?"
    answer: "Register your app's package name or bundle ID under Apps in your Appwrite project, create an analytics property with the Mobile type, and install the Appwrite SDK for your platform. Then create a Tracking instance with your property ID, call start() when the app launches, and send a screen view each time a screen opens."
  - question: "Does Appwrite Analytics count screen views as pageviews?"
    answer: "Yes. Appwrite counts screen_view events as views, the same as pageview events on a website. On a mobile property, the dashboard shows them as Screen views, and they fill the Screens/session figure and the entry and exit screens of each session."
  - question: "How does Appwrite Analytics measure time spent in an app?"
    answer: "Tracking sends an app_backgrounded event when the app goes to the background, with engagementTime set to the seconds it was in the foreground. The dashboard shows the average engagement time per session."
  - question: "Can one analytics property track both iOS and Android?"
    answer: "Yes. Each platform sends its events under its own hostname, such as ios or android. The Platforms tab of the Technology card splits the traffic by platform, and selecting a row filters the whole dashboard to that platform."
  - question: "Which Appwrite SDKs can send analytics events from a mobile app?"
    answer: "The Flutter, Android, Apple, and React Native SDKs all include a Tracking class that sends install, lifecycle, screen view, and custom events. Flutter also includes the TrackingObserver for routes. Any other platform can send events through the REST API."
  - question: "Does Appwrite Analytics count app installs?"
    answer: "Yes. start() sends first_open once per install and app_updated on the first launch after a new version. The Installs card on the dashboard counts both."
---

Appwrite Analytics shows how people use your mobile app: which screens they open, how long they stay, and what they do on each screen. One property can collect the events of your iOS and Android apps, so one dashboard shows both platforms side by side.

This tutorial adds Appwrite Analytics to an app built with Flutter, Kotlin on Android, SwiftUI on iOS, or React Native. Each code step has a tab for each platform. At the end, the app reports installs, screen views, time in the app, and custom events, and the property's dashboard shows them by screen and by platform.

# How app events appear on the dashboard

Appwrite Analytics records each event against a URL. An app sends `app://` with the platform and the screen, such as `app://ios/product`. The dashboard reads the platform as the hostname and the screen as the page path. The `Tracking` class in each Appwrite SDK sends these events:

| Event | When `Tracking` sends it |
| --- | --- |
| `first_open` | On the first launch after the app is installed |
| `app_updated` | On the first launch after an update, with the `previousVersion` and `version` props |
| `app_foregrounded` | When the app comes to the foreground |
| `app_backgrounded` | When the app goes to the background, with the seconds it was in the foreground |
| `screen_view` | When a screen opens, with the screen name in the `screen` prop |

Appwrite counts `screen_view` as a view, the same as `pageview` on a website. The seconds in `app_backgrounded` fill the Engagement time figure.

# Register your app in your project

Appwrite accepts events from an app only when its package name or bundle ID belongs to an app in your project.

1. In the Appwrite Console, open your project and go to **Apps**.
2. Select **Add app**.
3. Select your platform: **Android**, **Apple**, **Flutter**, or **React Native**. For Apple, Flutter, and React Native, also select the target, such as **Android** or **iOS**. Then select **Continue**.
4. Enter a name and your app's package name or bundle ID, such as `com.example.acmestore`.
5. Select **Register and continue**.

Repeat these steps for each target of the app, for example once for Android and once for iOS.

# Create an analytics property

![The Create property page with a name, the Mobile type, and the Flutter SDK selected](/images/blog/add-appwrite-analytics-to-your-mobile-app/create-property.avif)

A property collects the events of one app or website, and each property has its own dashboard. Use one property for all platforms of an app, so that the dashboard can compare them.

1. Go to **Analytics** and select **Create property**.
2. Enter a **Name**, such as `Acme mobile app`.
3. Under **Type**, select **Mobile**, and leave **Domain** empty.
4. Under **SDK**, select **Flutter** for a Flutter app, or **REST** for Android, iOS, and React Native. This choice only changes the install snippet that the Console shows next.
5. Select **Create property**.

The Console then shows the install steps for the property, with your property ID. Keep the page open. It confirms the first event that your app sends.

# Install the SDK

Add the Appwrite SDK for your platform:

{% tabs %}
{% tabsitem #flutter title="Flutter" %}

Add the `appwrite` package to your app:

```sh
flutter pub add appwrite
```

On Android, the SDK needs `minSdk` 24 or later and core library desugaring. Set both in `android/app/build.gradle.kts`:

```kotlin
android {
    defaultConfig {
        minSdk = 24
    }
    compileOptions {
        isCoreLibraryDesugaringEnabled = true
    }
}

dependencies {
    coreLibraryDesugaring("com.android.tools:desugar_jdk_libs:2.1.4")
}
```

{% /tabsitem %}

{% tabsitem #android title="Android (Kotlin)" %}

Add the Appwrite SDK to `app/build.gradle.kts`. The screen view example also uses Navigation Compose:

```kotlin
dependencies {
    implementation("io.appwrite:sdk-for-android:31.4.0")
    implementation("androidx.navigation:navigation-compose:2.10.2")
}
```

The SDK needs `compileSdk` 37 or later.

{% /tabsitem %}

{% tabsitem #apple title="Apple" %}

In Xcode, select **File** > **Add Package Dependencies**. Enter the package URL, and add the **Appwrite** library to your app target:

```text
https://github.com/appwrite/sdk-for-apple
```

{% /tabsitem %}

{% tabsitem #react-native title="React Native" %}

In an Expo app, install the SDK with the file system module and the URL polyfill that it uses:

```sh
npx expo install react-native-appwrite expo-file-system react-native-url-polyfill
```

The polyfill must load before any SDK code runs. The root layout in the screen view step imports it on its first line.

{% /tabsitem %}
{% /tabs %}

# Start tracking

Create one `Tracking` instance for the whole app with your endpoint, project ID, and property ID, and call `start()` when the app launches. Copy the endpoint and the project ID from your project's **Settings** page.

{% multicode %}
```client-flutter
// lib/main.dart
import 'package:appwrite/appwrite.dart';
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

final client = Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>');

final tracking = Tracking(Analytics(client), '<PROPERTY_ID>');

void main() {
  tracking.start();
  runApp(const AcmeApp());
}
```

```client-android-kotlin
// AcmeApp.kt
import android.app.Application
import io.appwrite.Client
import io.appwrite.Tracking
import io.appwrite.services.Analytics

class AcmeApp : Application() {
    lateinit var tracking: Tracking

    override fun onCreate() {
        super.onCreate()

        val client = Client(this)
            .setEndpoint("https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1")
            .setProject("<PROJECT_ID>")

        tracking = Tracking(Analytics(client), "<PROPERTY_ID>")
        tracking.start()
    }
}
```

```client-apple
// AcmeApp.swift
import Appwrite
import SwiftUI

let tracking: Tracking = {
    let client = Client()
        .setEndpoint("https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1")
        .setProject("<PROJECT_ID>")

    return Tracking(Analytics(client), propertyId: "<PROPERTY_ID>")
}()

@main
struct AcmeApp: App {
    init() {
        tracking.start()
    }

    var body: some Scene {
        WindowGroup {
            ContentView()
        }
    }
}
```

```client-react-native
// src/lib/analytics.ts
import { Analytics, Client, Tracking } from 'react-native-appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>')
    .setPlatform('com.example.acmestore');

export const tracking = new Tracking(new Analytics(client), '<PROPERTY_ID>');

tracking.start();
```
{% /multicode %}

`start()` sends `first_open` or `app_updated` and turns on the two lifecycle events, so the app reports installs and time in the app with no other code. On Android, register the class in `AndroidManifest.xml` with `android:name=".AcmeApp"` on the `<application>` element. In React Native, `setPlatform()` takes the package name or bundle ID registered under **Apps**.

# Track screen views

Send a `screen_view` each time a screen opens. Hook it into your navigation, so that new screens send their views with no extra code:

{% multicode %}
```client-flutter
// lib/main.dart
class AcmeApp extends StatelessWidget {
  const AcmeApp({super.key});

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return MaterialApp(
      navigatorObservers: [TrackingObserver(tracking)],
      routes: {
        '/': (context) => const HomeScreen(),
        '/product': (context) => const ProductScreen(),
      },
    );
  }
}
```

```client-android-kotlin
// MainActivity.kt
import android.os.Bundle
import androidx.activity.ComponentActivity
import androidx.activity.compose.setContent
import androidx.compose.runtime.LaunchedEffect
import androidx.navigation.compose.NavHost
import androidx.navigation.compose.composable
import androidx.navigation.compose.rememberNavController

class MainActivity : ComponentActivity() {
    override fun onCreate(savedInstanceState: Bundle?) {
        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState)
        val tracking = (application as AcmeApp).tracking

        setContent {
            val navController = rememberNavController()

            LaunchedEffect(navController) {
                navController.currentBackStackEntryFlow.collect { entry ->
                    entry.destination.route?.let { tracking.screenView(it) }
                }
            }

            NavHost(navController, startDestination = "home") {
                composable("home") { HomeScreen(onOpenProduct = { navController.navigate("product") }) }
                composable("product") { ProductScreen() }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

```client-apple
// ContentView.swift
import SwiftUI

struct ContentView: View {
    var body: some View {
        NavigationStack {
            NavigationLink("Open product") {
                ProductView()
            }
            .onAppear { tracking.screenView("home") }
        }
    }
}

struct ProductView: View {
    var body: some View {
        Text("Canvas backpack")
            .onAppear { tracking.screenView("product") }
    }
}
```

```client-react-native
// src/app/_layout.tsx
import 'react-native-url-polyfill/auto';

import { useEffect } from 'react';
import { Stack, usePathname } from 'expo-router';

import { tracking } from '@/lib/analytics';

export default function RootLayout() {
    const pathname = usePathname();

    useEffect(() => {
        tracking.screenView(pathname);
    }, [pathname]);

    return <Stack />;
}
```
{% /multicode %}

`HomeScreen` and `ProductScreen` stand for your own screens. Each platform reports a screen again when the user returns to it: `TrackingObserver` on every named route change in Flutter, the back stack flow after every navigation on Android, `onAppear` on iOS, and `usePathname()` from Expo Router in React Native.

# Track custom events

Screen views show where people go. Custom events record what they do, such as adding a product to the cart or completing a purchase. Send each event where the action completes, with the screen that it belongs to:

{% multicode %}
```client-flutter
tracking.event(
  'add_to_cart',
  url: '/product',
  props: {'product': 'canvas-backpack'},
);
```

```client-android-kotlin
tracking.event(
    "add_to_cart",
    url = "/product",
    props = mapOf("product" to "canvas-backpack"),
)
```

```client-apple
tracking.event("add_to_cart", url: "/product", props: ["product": "canvas-backpack"])
```

```client-react-native
tracking.track('add_to_cart', {
    url: '/product',
    props: { product: 'canvas-backpack' },
});
```
{% /multicode %}

Give each event a lowercase `snake_case` name for a finished action, such as `purchase_completed` or `review_posted`. Keep personal data, such as email addresses, out of event props.

# Confirm the first event

![The install page in the Console confirms that the first event from the app arrived](/images/blog/add-appwrite-analytics-to-your-mobile-app/event-received.avif)

Run your app on a device or an emulator. Within a few seconds, the install page in the Console shows **Event received** with the first event. Select **Open dashboard** to see the session.

# Read your app on the dashboard

![The Analytics dashboard for a mobile app with active users, sessions, screen views, and a chart for the last 30 days](/images/blog/add-appwrite-analytics-to-your-mobile-app/dashboard.avif)

Each property has its own dashboard, and a mobile property uses app terms. The top row shows active users, sessions, screen views, events, screens per session, session length, and engagement time for the selected date range. The dashboard opens filtered to **Humans only**, so automated test devices stay out of the numbers.

The **Push notifications** card fills in when the app reports notifications with `pushReceived()` and `pushOpened()`. The **online** count in the property header shows the people who used the app in the last five minutes.

## Count installs and updates

![The Installs card with new installs and updates for the last 30 days](/images/blog/add-appwrite-analytics-to-your-mobile-app/installs.avif)

The **Installs** card counts the `first_open` and `app_updated` events that `start()` sends. It shows how many people installed the app in the date range, and how many moved to a new version.

## Find the screens people use most

![The Screens card lists the app's screens by path, with the home, product, and search screens at the top](/images/blog/add-appwrite-analytics-to-your-mobile-app/screens.avif)

The **Screens** card lists your screens by path. **Top screens** ranks all screens, **Entry screens** shows the screen where each session starts, and **Exit screens** shows the screen where it ends. The lifecycle and install events do not belong to a screen, so the card lists them under `/`.

## Compare iOS and Android

![The Platforms tab of the Technology card splits the users between iOS and Android](/images/blog/add-appwrite-analytics-to-your-mobile-app/platforms.avif)

The **Platforms** tab of the **Technology** card splits your traffic by platform, because each platform sends its events under its own hostname, such as `ios` or `android`. Select a row to filter the whole dashboard to one platform, and every card then describes only the users of that platform.

